WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.584
A slide fills with words.

2
00:00:02.634 --> 00:00:06.427
You stop listening to read, and you fall behind on both.

3
00:00:06.477 --> 00:00:09.575
Here's how to spot an overloaded slide and fix it.

4
00:00:10.075 --> 00:00:13.392
When everything is written down, nothing looks important.

5
00:00:13.442 --> 00:00:19.420
And in a video, the slide only stays up while the narration runs, so viewers pause or give up.

6
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.345
Try the glance test.

7
00:00:21.395 --> 00:00:23.829
Look for three seconds, then look away.

8
00:00:23.879 --> 00:00:25.780
Can you say what it was about?

9
00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.638
Your headline should make a point, not a label.

10
00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.324
Keep body text at 24pt or larger.

11
00:00:32.374 --> 00:00:35.331
Watch for two ideas fighting on one slide.

12
00:00:35.381 --> 00:00:39.199
And if you could read it aloud as your script, move that text.

13
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.981
The words you cut don't disappear.

14
00:00:42.031 --> 00:00:49.576
Explanations go into speaker notes, reference detail goes in a handout, and one crowded slide becomes two or three clear ones.

15
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.858
Take a leave policy slide.

16
00:00:51.908 --> 00:00:54.842
Before: a label and five dense bullets.

17
00:00:54.892 --> 00:00:59.905
After: request leave two weeks ahead, through the HR portal, with three simple steps.

18
00:01:01.155 --> 00:01:08.340
The headline makes the point, the graphic shows the steps, and the narration carries the detail about accrual and reply times.

19
00:01:09.555 --> 00:01:12.942
The slide shows the point; your voice explains it.

20
00:01:12.992 --> 00:01:17.946
On Pro, AI directs the video, landing each key point on its word.

21
00:01:17.996 --> 00:01:21.198
Start with Basic at powerpoint2vid.com.

